whereas generative AI facilitates data augmentation, feature representation and synthesis of multimodal clinical information to strengthen predictive model robustness and clinical decision-making. In addition, the AI technologies applied in radiation oncology can be divided in terms of scale into narrow and general AI, which differ in the sense that narrow AI is specialized for a single task whereas general AI models (referred to as foundation models) can be adapted to multiple tasks. Foundation models are typically large-scale AI models that are trained on a vast amount of often unlabelled, diverse data using techniques such as self-supervised and/or weak-supervised learning, enabling their adjustment for many different tasks. Multimodal foundation models, including VLMs that jointly process imaging and textual information, enable integration of radiological images, treatment planning data and clinical text. Generative AI models can produce hallucinations, which constitute an important safety concern in clinical applications24.
Agentic AI and digital twins
The emergence of digital twins is a key companion development to AI. The US National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine has formally defined digital twins as “a set of virtual information constructs that mimics the structure, context and behaviour of a natural, engineered or social system (or system-of-systems), is dynamically updated with data from its physical twin, has a predictive capability, and informs decisions that realize value”27. In this symbiotic relationship28, AI provides digital twins with computing cognition (a ‘brain’) whereas digital twins provide AI with an engineered context (an ‘environment’). Agentic AI builds on generative AI and LLMs not only by producing content such as structured clinical notes, treatment summaries or protocol recommendations, but also by using that information to carry
Key Points
• Artificial intelligence (AI) is currently transforming radiation oncology workflows and patient care through a growing number of commercially available and Food and Drug Administration-approved tools.
• AI is transforming the defined roles of radiation oncology care providers by automating routine tasks and supporting predictive and clinical decision-making, while expert human oversight remains essential across AI-enabled workflows.
• Many ongoing clinical trials are evaluating the technical performance and feasibility of AI-enabled applications for radiation oncology, and clinical research is now starting to address the safety, efficacy and clinical value of these tools.
• Despite substantial progress, several challenges hinder the effective implementation of AI tools into routine radiation oncology workflows, and the field needs practical recommendations to support responsible clinical adoption of these tools.
• The convergence of digital twins, generative AI and foundation models is poised to shape the next era of AI-enabled radiotherapy.
Introduction
The practice of radiation oncology involves complex, data-intensive clinical workflows encompassing heterogeneous sources of information at every stage — from initial consultation and imaging to treatment planning, quality assurance and delivery of radiotherapy. The aim is to ensure that patients receive the prescribed radiation treatment safely and accurately. These steps can vary and involve several stages of sophisticated human–machine interactions and decision-making, such as chart review, machine-specific and patient-related quality assurance, tumour and/or organ contouring, treatment planning, image guidance, management of respiratory motion, response modelling and prediction of outcomes1. The body of experience in this field grows with every newly treated patient, creating a wealth of information, which, combined with the error-averse nature of radiation oncology, makes this medical specialty an ideal setting for leveraging the unique capabilities of artificial intelligence (AI)2–4. In this Review, we present examples of successful AI applications in radiation oncology. We further delve into the current challenges of successful clinical implementation of AI, machine learning and digital twins in this specialty by outlining key requirements and exploring strategies that integrate scientific discovery with engineering to address these obstacles, including the need for training programmes to enable health-care professionals to meet the requirements of the AI era. We also discuss the broader implications of using AI tools in radiation oncology, such as the need to ensure safe and optimal use of AI and machine learning in order to optimize the quality of care and, ultimately, patient outcomes.
