Instead of relying on static datasets or single timepoint predictions, digital twins continuously integrate multimodal AI data, including imaging, electronic health records, dosimetry, biomarkers and patient-reported outcomes (PROs), to simulate response to and toxicities from treatment over time36,37. Early implementations of digital twin models relevant to radiation oncology have demonstrated feasibility in generating patient-specific predictive models of response to radiotherapy (Fig. 2). For example, digital twin frameworks integrating tumour growth dynamics with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) data have been used to optimize radiotherapy regimens for patients with high-grade gliomas33,34. Another example includes the application of digital twin frameworks, personalized adaptive proton stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) with efficient selection of a treatment plan selection and reduced target volume set-up uncertainty in patients with prostate cancer35. In this new paradigm, agentic AI can interact with the digital twin to test therapeutic strategies, optimize adaptive radiotherapy plans, predict adverse events and personalize clinical decision-making. Together, agentic AI and digital twins have the potential to enable proactive, continuous learning radiation oncology workflows, improve outcomes and inform the design of future clinical trials (Fig. 3).
AI applications across the radiation oncology workflow
AI has potential applications across the radiotherapy workflow, both to provide support for the automation of clinical tasks and as a predictive tool for clinical decision-making to deliver treatment in an adaptive manner (Fig. 1). Generative AI is generally used in supportive applications, whereas adaptive applications typically rely on predictive AI models. Automated approaches have enabled substantial gains in tasks such as organ segmentation, synthetic image generation, treatment
planning, imaging guidance and quality assurance, thereby improving efficiency and consistency of care. Auto-segmentation has reduced contouring time from hours to minutes while achieving expert-level agreement for many disease sites38–40. AI-assisted treatment planning has improved consistency in plan quality and reduced planning time through automated optimization41. In image guidance, AI-based deformable image registration and synthetic computed tomography (CT) generation have facilitated adaptive radiotherapy by improving registration accuracy and enabling MRI-only treatment planning42. AI-driven quality assurance systems have further enhanced workflow efficiency by automatically detecting contouring, planning and delivery errors before treatment, reducing manual workload while maintaining patient safety. Together, these advances have improved efficiency, reproducibility and consistency across the radiation oncology workflow. Predictive models enable risk stratification, prediction of response to and toxicities from treatment, and adaptive treatment planning. These applications span the radiation oncology workflow, including automation of tasks (such as contouring, treatment planning, quality assurance and imaging guidance), analyses of response to treatment (personalized prediction of outcomes, re-irradiation and adaptative strategies), emerging multimodal approaches (for example, via integration of radiotherapy with systemic therapies such as immune checkpoint inhibitors in radioimmunotherapy) and, in the past few years, advances in generative AI and foundation models for treatment planning and delivery43. Advances include multimodal foundation models capable of jointly learning from radiological images, pathology slides, genomic data and clinical text to improve outcome prediction, and from clinical text to improve outcome prediction and clinical decision support. In parallel, LLMs have demonstrated promise for treatment planning assistance, contour review, t