tions and the envisioned fully integrated, dynamically updated patient models. Unlike cardiovascular applications, such as coronary CT-derived fractional flow reserve, in which patient-specific computational models have been incorporated into clinical decision-making, most digital twin models in radiology, radiotherapy and theranostics remain at the framework developmental stage46–48.
Synthetic imaging and multimodal AI. Synthetic imaging and data integration with multimodal AI are key areas of radiotherapy currently undergoing substantial advances. AI-driven synthesis of medical images has emerged as an essential component of clinical decisionmaking, particularly in settings requiring multiple imaging modalities41. Beyond generation of synthetic CT images, AI-based approaches have been developed for synthetic imaging across multiple modalities, including MRI and positron emission tomography, and for crossmodality synthesis translation between CT, MRI and positron emission tomography49. In particular, the generation of synthetic CT images from MRI data has received substantial attention for radiotherapy planning because it enables accurate dose calculation without the need for additional CT scans50,51. Clinical applications of MRI-derived synthetic CT have been most extensively investigated in prostate cancer and intracranial radiotherapy. More recently, studies have expanded to head and neck, and pelvic malignancies. These approaches have the potential to reduce patient burden and streamline workflows while maintaining dosimetric fidelity. AI-based MRI-to-CT synthesis demonstrated superior accuracy compared with conventional techniques such as atlas-based registration52–54. For example, a deep convolutional neural network (CNN) developed for patients with brain tumours reduced the mean absolute error from 94.5 ± 17.8 Hounsfield units (HU) with an atlas-based approach to 84.8 ± 17.3 HU (increased Pearson correlation coefficient 0.896–0.906)55. More broadly, cross-modality synthesis translation facilitates the integration of complementary imaging information for enhancing target delineation, treatment planning and response assessment, while reducing reliance on multiple imaging acquisitions. These techniques address practical challenges associated with multimodal imaging acquisition, including cost, patient burden and image registration inaccuracies. By leveraging generativedeep learning approaches, such as GANs and diffusion models, synthetic imaging approaches have demonstrated improved spatial accuracy and consistency compared with conventional approaches, although many applications remain investigational50,54. Impending challenges with
