Automated segmentation and contouring. Automated segmentation and contouring of tumours and organs at risk constitutes one of the most mature and clinically adopted AI applications in radiotherapy. Deep learning architectures, particularly U-Net-based models, have demonstrated excellent accuracy in delineating anatomical structures across multiple disease locations, including the head and neck, thoracic, abdominal and pelvic regions. Contemporary deep learning auto-segmentation models have achieved Dice similarity coefficients frequently exceeding 0.85–0.90 for many organs of interest, approaching typical levels of interobserver agreement among expert clinicians57. This automation reduces interobserver variability and substantially decreases contouring time, thereby improving workflow efficiency58. For example, in a blinded randomized clinical validation study involving 42 organs at risk, deep learning-based auto-segmentation reduced total contouring time by 76% (3.4 h to 0.7 h) for head and neck organs at risk59. Moreover, in the setting of adaptive radiotherapy, rapid auto-contouring enables frequent and timely reoptimization of treatment plans on the basis of anatomical changes. Despite these advances, physician oversight remains essential to ensure clinical accuracy, and to account for complex or atypical tumours. As such, automated segmentation is best viewed as an assistive tool within a human-in-the-loop framework. Automated segmentation has become an essential component of contemporary radiation oncology workflows with several commercial tools that can offer high-quality contouring performance60. Nevertheless, additional work needs to be done to reliably and accurately segment gross tumour volumes in contrast to organs of interest. Even in such a case, performance can depend on the algorithm and its implementation. In a prospective randomized trial with results published in 2024, AI-assisted heart contouring did not reduce time compared with standard manual contouring, underscoring the need to evaluate AI tools for their intended use in real-world clinical workflows61.
AI-driven treatment planning and dose optimization. Extending beyond segmentation, AI has also transformed treatment planning and dose optimization. Machine learning-based approaches, including KBP and deep learning models, enable the prediction of optimal dose distributions tailored to patient-specific anatomy. Conventional dose planning is an iterative and time-consuming process requiring repeated adjustments with the aim of optimizing and achieving target coverage while respecting dose to organs of interest. By contrast, AI-driven approaches can predict achievable dose distributions and streamline inverse planning, an automated process in which the prescribed target dose and organ constraints are predefined (as opposed to manually assigned and adjusted), reducing planning time while improving plan consistency. These models leverage prior high-quality plans to guide optimization and support reproducible, data-driven workflows. Morerecent approaches leverage deep learning, CNNs and GANs to predict three-dimensional (3D) voxel-wise dose distributions. For example, a deep learning model based on the U-net architecture and its variants can be used for 3D voxel-wise dose prediction for irradiation of prostate16 and head and neck17 cancers. Conversely, early implementations of KBP focused on leveraging historical plan data to improve efficiency and consistency62. An evaluation of ten KBP models integrated
within a fully automated treatment-planning workflow across ten distinct disease locations demonstrated that high-quality, clinically acceptable plans can be generated without routine manual optimization63. Upon review of the KBP-generated plans, physicians deemed that 88% of such plans were clinically acceptable without modification and 98% were acceptable after only minor edits, demonstrating the potential for robust automation across diverse disease locations63. Over the past few years, reinforcement learning has been applied to automate and improve inverse treatment-planning efficiency for brachytherapy19 and external-beam radiotherapy64,65. Within these models, those using deep reinforcement learning (DRL) have shown improved accuracy and efficiency compared with KBP, although most AI treatment-planning models are largely investigational66. Direct comparative studies between DRL and KBP based treatment planning remain limited, although the currently existing studies suggest that AI-based planning approaches can achieve plan quality comparable to or, in select settings, exceeding KBP while reducing planning time67. Collectively, these advances have the potential to shift radiation oncology towards more personalized, adaptive and data-driven treatment paradigms.
From KBP to adaptive and agentic systems. Building on these advances, contemporary AI approaches are increasingly integrating KBP and DRL with adaptive radiotherapy, ablative SBRT, re-irradiation and radioimmunotherapy frameworks, among others. AI-enabled planning systems now leverage multimodal data and auto-dose prediction to streamline inverse planning and personalize optimization objectives on the basis of patient-specific characteristics63. In parallel, adaptive radiotherapy incorporates real-time imaging and predictive analytics to identify when treatment modification is required, maintaining optimal target coverage while minimizing toxicity. Early clinical implementation of image-guided adaptive radiotherapy, such as MRI-guided adaptive workflows, has demonstrated the feasibility of modifying treatment plans in response to anatomical changes during the course of treatment68. Clinically, MRI-guided radiotherapy has provided a practical foundation for online adaptation: in a multi-institutional phase 2 trial testing MRI-guided on-table adaptive SBRT in 136 patients with borderline resectable and locally advanced pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, adaptive replanning was performed for the vast majority of delivered fractions (93%), demonstrating the feasibility of routine on-table adaptation at scale68. Additional prospective trials testing MRI-guided adaptive radiotherapy such as this one have demonstrated the feasibility of routine on-table adaptation while maintaining dosimetric quality and clinical workflow efficiency69. Furthermore, AI enhancement could support the transition from ‘single-shot’ planning to iterative decision support66. Together, these advances unify KBP, DRL-based planning and adaptive decision support into a cohesive framework that promises more efficient, data-driven and reproducible clinical workflows while preserving clinician oversight. The evolution of AI-enabled radiotherapy extends beyond static plan generation towards adaptive, continuously learning treatment systems. Building on these foundations, AI methods increasingly aim to integrate real-time imaging, delivered dose reconstruction and predictive modelling into structured decision-support frameworks, therefore enabling a shift towards closed-loop radiotherapy frameworks. Agentic AI does not necessarily imply autonomous clinical decision-making, but rather systems capable of monitoring inputs, identifying predefined trigger thresholds and initiating transparent,


