Motion management and image-guided radiotherapy. Intrafraction motion remains a substantial challenge in radiotherapy, particularly for thoracic and abdominal tumours affected by respiration and organ deformation. Researchers have developed AI-based motion-tracking and image-analysis techniques to estimate real-time tumour position using fluoroscopy, cone–beam CT, MRI and surface imaging. These models enable dynamic tumour tracking, respiratory gating and adaptive beam delivery, improving targeting accuracy while reducing unnecessary irradiation of surrounding healthy tissue. Contemporary image-guided motion-management strategies can account for respiratory-induced tumour motion that might exceed 10 mm in thoracic and upper abdominal malignancies, enabling more precise target localization and narrower treatment margins70. In MRI-guided radiotherapy systems, AI-driven segmentation and motion modelling further support continuous visualization and online adaptation, improving the precision of image-guided treatment delivery. These capabilities are essential for enabling real-time feedback with closed-loop radiotherapy systems and form an essential component of adaptive and digital twin-driven treatment paradigms.
Outcome prediction, toxicity modelling and longitudinal follow-up. Predictive analytics is a rapidly expanding domain of AI in radiation oncology, with models developed to forecast response to treatment, toxicity to non-malignant tissues, disease control and survival outcomes. In the past few years, AI-based outcome-prediction models have demonstrated clinically meaningful predictive performance, with several studies reporting area under the curve values >0.80 for toxicity, disease recurrence and survival end points71, although further validation is still warranted. Prediction models have traditionally relied on structured variables such as clinician-graded toxicities. However, the grading of adverse events outside of clinical trials is often inconsistently documented, and a large proportion of relevant information resides in unstructured clinical documentation and PROs. Advances in LLMs enable automated extraction and grading of adverse events from clinical notes. For example, in a benchmarking study with results published in 2025, LLMs showed high accuracy for binary calling of adverse effects and moderate agreement for grade-level classification, highlighting the potential of such models to enhance outcome capture at scale72. By leveraging multimodal inputs, including imaging features, dosimetric parameters, clinical variables and PROs, these models aim to support risk-adapted treatment strategies and personalized care73. For example, one model included 475 patients with early-stage non-smallcell lung cancer treated with SBRT and used longitudinally collected PROs together with clinical and demographic variables. The transformer model achieved a cross-validated area under the curve of 0.862 (c-index 0.753) for overall survival prediction and outperformed Cox and joint probability survival models73. AI-based models for the prediction of toxicities, for example, have demonstrated promise in identifying patients at elevated risk for radiation-induced complications, potentially enabling proactive mitigation strategies74,75. For example, a deep learning model developed with data from 784 patients with head and neck cancer enrolled in the RTOG 0522 trial integrated planning CT images, 3D dose distributions and salivary-gland contours to
predict grade ≥2 xerostomia at 12 months after radiotherapy75. Longitudinal outcome modelling further enables continuous learning from real-world clinical data, providing a foundation for adaptive treatment approaches and the future development of dynamic AI–digital twin paired frameworks.
AI in patient education and clinical communication. LLMs have also been evaluated as tools for patient education in radiation oncology. In a prospective study, clinicians as well as patients with meningioma who had undergone radiotherapy highly rated LLM-generated responses to disease-related questions in terms of relevance, clarity and correctness76. Of note, patients expressed moderate-to-high trust in the information and willingness to use such tools for future education76.
AI for handling clinical documentation and administrative burden. Ambient AI scribes are voice-enabled AI tools used in health care to generate clinical notes. Evaluations of these tools have demonstrated that the use of AI for documentation purposes can reduce the cognitive load on clinicians and improve workflow efficiency, reducing time spent on clinical documentation outside scheduled working hours by ~30% and improving self-reported clinician efficiency, cognitive workload and satisfaction77,78. Qualitative studies using semi-structured interviews across multiple specialties and practice settings confirm these observations79. However, clinical documentation continues to require physician review and editing to ensure accuracy80. Administrative processes such as prior authorization contribute to treatment delays. AI systems can assist by generating standardized, guideline-concordant prior authorization documentation, supporting medical necessity and streamlining administrative workflows. In this context, AI functions as a workforce multiplier rather than an autonomous decision-maker81.
Machine-specific and patient-related quality assurance. Quality assurance frameworks for AI in radiation oncology have evolved across both machine-specific and patient-specific domains82. Several studies highlight that both conventional approaches and deep learning-based architectures (such as CNNs, recurrent neural networks and variational autoencoders) need to be integrated to predict beam characteristics, detect mechanical errors and estimate dosimetric differences in intensity-modulated radiotherapy and volumetric modulated arc therapy plans83–85. Specifically, gamma passing rate (GPR) is a quantitative dosimetric metric for patient-related quality assurance that enables comparison of the planned dose distribution and the measured delivered dose distribution. GPR is widely used to validate intensity-modulated radiotherapy and volumetric modulated arc therapy plans, detect multileaf collimation errors, assess delivery accuracy and compare predicted versus measured quality assurance; however, high GPR values do not guarantee the absence of clinically relevant errors. Reported mean absolute errors for GPR prediction fall within the acceptable threshold of 2–3%, suggesting that AI-based quality assurance approaches maintain safety standards while reducing measurement burden86,87.
Outcomes modelling and survivorship care. Modelling of treatment outcomes is a key application of AI in radiation oncology88. Emerging models of survivorship and outcomes integrate multimodal data streams, including dosimetry, imaging radiomics and clinical variables, as well as longitudinal follow-up timepoints to dynamically estimate individual risk89. Meta-analyses suggests that hybrid models combining radiomics and clinical features outperform single-modality


