Clinical implementation
The implementation of AI in clinical practice involves multiple stages, including model validation, acceptance testing, commissioning, deployment, quality assurance, quality control and continuous performance monitoring. Despite rapid advances, challenges remain for the implementation of AI applications into routine workflows for radiation oncology; technical, operational and governance-related barriers include data heterogeneity, workflow integration and limited model generalizability across institutions92,93. Several frameworks have been proposed to guide the safe and effective integration of AI into radiation oncology, outlining structured pathways from model development to clinical deployment and monitoring94,95. These frameworks include recommendations for prospective validation, implementation and ongoing quality assurance within the radiotherapy workflow96–99. In parallel, regulatory oversight has a crucial role in ensuring that AI-enabled tools meet established safety and performance standards, with evolving frameworks and guidance from regulatory authorities in the USA, European Union (EU), UK and other regions.
Regulation of AI devices
The regulatory landscape for AI in health care is rapidly evolving, reflecting the need to balance innovation with patient safety and clinical accountability. In March 2024, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) released updated guidance outlining key principles for the development and regulation of AI-enabled medical devices, particularly software as a medical device (SaMD)100. AI and machine learning-based software developed with the intention to treat, diagnose, cure, mitigate or prevent disease or other medical conditions is considered a medical device under the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and is commonly classified as SaMD by both the FDA and the International Medical Device Regulators Forum. The majority of these tools are cleared as Class II devices via the 510(k) pathway, which means that the product is ‘substantially equivalent’ to a legally marketed predicate device and is marketed for clinical use within specified indications. Machine learning-based SaMDs can be further adapted after deployment using real-world data, thereby enabling ongoing improvement of their performance. However, post-market changes can result in outputs different than those originally cleared for a set of inputs100, highlighting the need for regulatory frameworks that accommodate controlled product evolution and a lifecycle-based approach to enable more personalized product customization services driven by customer demands (in this case, from health care) over the lifecycle of the product101. Despite progress in guidance development over the past few years, no federal legislation currently governs the use of AI in health care in the USA, which is largely decentralized and has a fragmented regulatory oversight. Instead, a combination of federal guidance and state-level initiatives has emerged. For example, the AI Act of May 2024 in Colorado
(SB 24-205) addresses algorithmic accountability and patient-data privacy, whereas in Illinois, the AI Systems Act mandates coverage decisions for AI-driven health care. These initiatives demonstrate the growing recognition of the need for transparency, accountability and post-market surveillance. In contrast to the FDA, the EU has adopted a more-centralized regulatory approach through the Medical Device Regulation (EU MDR) of 2017 and the EU Artificial Intelligence Act in 2024102, which classify AI systems used with diagnostic, monitoring and clinical decision-support purposes as high-risk medical devices. This framework imposes strict requirements for validation, risk management, transparency and continuous post-market surveillance, with a strong emphasis on human oversight and auditability. Professional societies also have an increasingly important role in defining best practices for the integration of AI into clinical workflows. In a joint guideline issued in June 2024, the American Association of Physicists in Medicine and the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology outline a comprehensive framework for the validation and clinical deployment of AI in radiotherapy97. They emphasize standardized validation, transparent reporting and model accessibility, reinforcing the importance of reproducibility and trust in clinicians. Globally, regulatory approaches continue to vary. For example, China has adopted a regulatory framework emphasizing security, risk monitoring and assessment, and regulatory oversight of AI. The Chinese Cybersecurity Law was amended in 2025 to incorporate provisions addressing AI development, ethical standards, risk monitoring and assessment, and safety supervision, with the amendments taking effect on 1 January 2026. Personal information, health data and other data referred to as important that are collected or generated in China must generally be stored within China’s borders. Moreover, post-market monitoring is government-led. The difference between this regulatory framework and those in other regions underscore the need for international harmonization of such frameworks, particularly as AI-enabled tools are deployed across diverse health-care systems. Taken together, current regulatory efforts reflect an evolving balance between fostering innovation and ensuring patient safety. As AI systems become more adaptive and are increasingly integrated into clinical decision-making, future regulatory models will need to shift towards lifecycle-based oversight models that emphasize continuous validation, real-world performance monitoring and human-inthe-loop supervision. Within this regulatory context, FDA-cleared AI-enabled medical devices constitute the current standard for clinically deployable tools, providing important insights into how AI is being translated into real-world radiation oncology practice. Importantly, not all AI software developed for research purposes is approved by regulatory authorities. This distinction between regulatory approval and research-grade AI models underscores the importance of evaluating clinically validated tools. FDA-cleared platforms are clinically validated tools with established safety and performance profiles. As of May 2026, the FDA Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Medical Devices List included 31 unique AI-enabled tools spanning all major stages of the radiation oncology workflow (Table 1 and Supplementary Table 1). This list of FDA-cleared AI-enabled devices reflects the fact that the AI applications currently used in radiation oncology focus primarily on workflow support functions rather than autonomous clinical decision-making. Deep learning-based approaches predominate, accounting for 58% of the AI-enabled radiation oncology current applications, particularly for image segmentation and synthetic image generation. Although less commonly used, machine learning and hybrid models are the


