Clinical trials
Following the increasing number of FDA-cleared AI tools for radiation oncology, rigorous clinical evaluation to establish their safety, effectiveness and role in real-world clinical settings is a crucial need. Whereas regulatory approval reflects technical performance and safety within defined parameters, clinical trials are the gold standard for evaluating the safety and efficacy of new medical tools — including AI tools — and whether they meaningfully improve workflow efficiency, decision-making and patient outcomes. As of May 2026, ClinicalTrials.gov listed 50 clinical trials evaluating AI applications across the radiation oncology workflow (Table 2 and Supplementary Table 2). Collectively, supportive and workflow-oriented applications — including contouring and segmentation, treatment planning, adaptive radiotherapy and image-processing workflows accounted for ~60% of identified trials, whereas predictive applications accounted for ~40%. Among individual application categories, outcome prediction was the most common, constituting ~36% of trials. A smaller proportion of studies focus on outcome prediction, toxicity assessment, symptom monitoring or personalized treatment adaptation. In addition, the majority of identified studies are interventional feasibility or workflow-evaluation trials (66% versus 34% being observational studies), with fewer assessing the effect of AI on patient-centred end points (Supplementary Table 2).
Synthetic clinical trials
Generative AI models are being explored as a means to address insufficient patient accrual in oncology clinical trials. A validation study using data from nine completed clinical trials involving patients with breast cancer demonstrated that sequential synthesis models (a generative
AI subtype) can simulate additional patients and replicate published treatment effects103. The investigators retrospectively simulated insufficient-accrual scenarios using these completed trial datasets to evaluate whether generative models could augment the missing patient population. Notably, the models reproduced treatment effects even when trained on datasets comprising ~60% of the original trial population, suggesting their potential to augment incomplete or underpowered trial datasets. Moreover, digital twins could have a relevant role in synthetic clinical trials44,104. In this context, virtual clinical trial validations of newly developed AI products could be conducted with digital twins and/or underpowered clinical trials could be augmented with accurate synthetic data to draw more meaningful conclusions about the performance of the AI tool being evaluated34. These applications highlight the broad potential of generative AI in radiation oncology; however, the clinical relevance of generative AI-based tools will ultimately depend on their ability to predict and optimize patient-specific outcomes. Outcomes modelling provides the unifying computational framework through which these diverse tools can be integrated into clinically actionable decision support (Fig. 3). In a clinical decision-support computational framework, a continuously updated patient-specific digital twin integrates longitudinal imaging, delivered dose and temporal anatomical changes across treatment fractions45,105,106. A set of modular AI agents operates on this shared representation. For example, a perception agent processes daily cone–beam CT data to update anatomy using segmentation and deformable image registration. A prediction agent forecasts interfraction anatomical variations and associated uncertainties. A planning agent generates candidate treatment plans using robust, scenario-based optimization across predicted anatomies. An evaluation agent computes dose distributions, constraint adherence, and risk of irradiation of adjacent tissue and quality metrics on both the current and predicted anatomies. A decision and governance agent selects the optimal clinical action (selecting or adapting a plan, or replanning) under uncertainty-aware and clinically constrained conditions. Finally, a learning agent updates the digital twin after each fraction on the basis
