Framework for AI-based outcome modelling
In radiation oncology, outcome modelling involves mapping the correlations between high-dimensional patient-specific inputs and clinically relevant end points. Unlike traditional radiobiological models, which rely primarily on dosimetric parameters, contemporary approaches integrate heterogeneous data spanning clinical characteristics, treatment variables, imaging features and biological information. This shift reflects an evolving understanding that the response to radiotherapy is governed by complex, multiscale interactions that cannot be captured by simplified analytical formulations. Contemporary AI-based outcome models are typically structured as modular, multimodal architectures in which each data modality is first encoded into a latent representation using modality-specific networks (such as CNNs for imaging or representation learning methods for biological data). For example, models developed in the past few
years have integrated CT imaging, 3D dose distributions and clinical variables to predict treatment-related toxicity, while other multiomodal approaches have incorporated imaging, dosimetric and biological and/or clinical features for risk prediction. These studies demonstrate the current feasibility of integrating heterogeneous patient-specific and treatment-specific data within a unified predictive framework, although most of the current evidence remains retrospective107,108. These representations are subsequently fused into a shared feature space, enabling the joint prediction of multiple clinically relevant end points, including tumour control, treatment-related toxicity, survival and PROs. Importantly, the ability to model competing or complementary end points within a unified framework is a considerable advance over traditional approaches that treat these outcomes independently. From a clinical perspective, outcome models support three principal functions in radiation oncology: (1) treatment planning by enabling optimization of tumour control while minimizing side effects; (2) personalization through individualized risk estimation; and (3) adaptation by updating predictions over the course of treatment. However, despite promising performance in retrospective studies, the implementation of these models in clinical practice remains limited by challenges related to data heterogeneity, generalizability and lack of prospective validation. To address these limitations, a rigorous development and validation pipeline is required, encompassing standardized data curation, robust training and fine-tuning strategies, and both internal and external validation. In addition, consideration of interpretability, uncertainty estimation and algorithmic bias is key to ensure safe and equitable deployment. Looking forward, the integration of longitudinal data, causal modelling and real-time learning systems is expected to transform outcome modelling from a static predictive tool into a dynamic component of adaptive, personalized radiotherapy, forming the computational foundation for digital twin-enabled clinical decision-making.
