Stage 2: data curation and harmonization, and cohort definition. Patient cohorts are defined on the basis of treatment characteristics and follow-up, with careful attention to inclusion and exclusion criteria. Data are curated to ensure quality, completeness and representativeness, including handling of missing data, standardization (using parameters such as Z-score or methods such as min–max normalization), harmonization across sources (for example, Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events versions) and mitigation of bias in under-represented populations. Nevertheless, using uncurated real-world datasets when developing large-scale foundation models using techniques such as self-supervised and/or weak-supervised learning remains a valuable approach, as discussed earlier.
Step 3: model development and optimization. Datasets are partitioned into training, validation and test sets to mitigate data leakage and minimize overfitting. Cross-validation strategies and class-balancing techniques (such as the synthetic minority over-sampling technique) are applied within the training set. Feature engineering or automated representation learning is performed, and models are developed with optimization of architecture and hyperparameters using validation data.
Step 4: validation, evaluation and interpretability. Model performance is rigorously evaluated using internal and external validation cohorts, with emphasis on generalizability, calibration and reproducibility. Discrimination, calibration and clinical utility metrics are assessed across clinically relevant subgroups. Importantly, this evaluation must extend beyond aggregate performance to include robustness to distributional shift, with explicit assessment of out-of-bounds or out-of-distribution inputs relative to the training data. Models should be tested for their ability to detect and appropriately respond to such inputs, including uncertainty estimation or abstention mechanisms (that is, declining to make a prediction) when predictions might be unreliable. Interpretability approaches109 (including model-specific methods, model-agnostic methods such as local interpretable modelagnostic explanations (LIME) or Shapley additive explanations (SHAP), or saliency mapping) are incorporated to support clinical transparency, facilitate human–AI interaction and ensure that model behaviour aligns with clinical reasoning.
Step 5: bias assessment, safety and clinical integration.Systematicbias and fairness are evaluated across demographic and clinical subgroups110. Safeguards such as out-of-distribution detection and uncertainty estimation are incorporated to prevent inappropriate use of the model. Models that meet these criteria are integrated into clinical workflows with ongoing monitoring, regulatory oversight and human-in-the-loop decision-making to ensure safe and effective deployment.
Current challenges and recommendations
The clinical implementation of AI in radiation oncology is accompanied by a broad range of technical, methodological, ethical and
operational challenges, which span infrastructure limitations, data quality and accessibility, model development and validation, regulatory oversight and workforce readiness. Addressing these barriers is essential to ensure safe, effective and equitable integration of AI into clinical workflows (Table 3). Explainable AI approaches can provide clinicians with interpretable information about the factors contributing to model outputs, supporting evaluation of AI-assisted decisions109,111. Taken together, these challenges are not theoretical, but instead are increasingly being observed in early clinical deployments of AI-enabled contouring, adaptive radiotherapy and outcome prediction systems in radiation oncology.
Technical requirements
In terms of software and hardware requirements, it is important to distinguish between the development and deployment phases. Model development often relies on high-performance computing and parallel processing, whereas clinical deployment prioritizes efficiency, reliability and real-time usability. For example, auto-contouring systems that require substantial manual correction can paradoxically increase clinician workload despite high segmentation accuracy. To address these challenges, AI tools should be designed with workflow integration as a primary objective, leveraging vendor-neutral architectures and standardized interfaces, although proprietary vendor-provided ecosystems that integrate AI solutions might need to be prioritized, particularly in smaller non-academic centres. Prospective evaluation within clinical workflows is essential to ensure usability and minimize disruption.
Data engineering
The performance and safety of AI models in radiation oncology are fundamentally determined by the quality, representativeness and structure of training data. Radiotherapy datasets often vary across institutions owing to differences in imaging protocols, contouring conventions, dose-prescription practices and toxicity documentation. The resulting heterogeneity can negatively affect model generalizability. A key challenge is that training datasets might not adequately represent the diversity of clinical practice, resulting in biased or non-generalizable models. In radiation oncology, the use of non-representative models can manifest as reduced performance of segmentation or toxicity-prediction models when applied to under-represented patient subgroups, tumour locations or institutions. To address these limitations, efforts have focused on establishing standardized data ontologies for structures and outcomes, rigorous data curation pipelines, and multicentre data-sharing initiatives112,113. Federated learning has also emerged as a promising approach, which enables distributing model training across institutions without direct data sharing, thereby improving scalability while preserving patient privacy114–116.
Model development validation
Robust validation strategies are essential to ensure safe implementation of AI models into radiation oncology practice. Many models demonstrate robust internal performance; however, external validation frequently reveals reduced accuracy owing to dataset shift, including differences in imaging protocols, contouring practices and patient populations across institutions. Examples include toxicity-prediction models and automated segmentation systems117–119. As a result, multicentre validation, calibration testing and subgroup analyses are key components of model evaluation. Furthermore, prospective studies embedded within clinical workflows are needed to assess not only


