Ethics and regulations
In radiation oncology, AI systems introduce important ethical and regulatory considerations, particularly given their role in high-stakes decision-making such as target delineation and dose optimization. Regulatory agencies, such as the FDA, have a central role in evaluating the safety and effectiveness of AI-based medical devices; however, regulatory clearance alone does not guarantee consistent performance across clinical environments. In radiation oncology, in which workflows vary considerably across institutions, additional local validation is often required before deployment. Moreover, adaptive or continuously learning AI systems introduce new challenges for regulatory oversight, including version control, transparency and post-deployment monitoring. These issues highlight the need for evolving regulatory frameworks that can accommodate dynamic AI systems used in clinical practice. The FDA’s Predetermined Change Control Plan framework for AI-enabled device software functions is an evolving approach that allows manufacturers to prospectively specify anticipated model modifications, methods for their development and validation, and approaches for assessing their effect120. This framework is intended to enable controlled iterative modification of AI-enabled devices while maintaining reasonable assurance of safety and effectiveness throughout the lifecycle of the product. Complementary Good Machine Learning Practice principles further emphasize representative datasets, independent testing, human–AI team performance and monitoring of deployed models across the total lifecycle of the product121.
Governance
Governance systems create a legal framework to monitor and ensure safe and responsible implementation of AI in radiation oncology. As the role of AI continues to grow, the responsibility for AI-assisted decision-making becomes increasingly distributed across multiple stakeholders, including clinicians, physicists, administrators and vendors. A report on the governance structure of a single institution in oncology (not restricted to radiation oncology only)122 identified the following minimum components: (1) presence of a multidisciplinary governance committee; (2) defining the intake process for new AI model ideas; (3) a risk-assessment framework; (4) a model registry; (5) access to vendor monitoring data; (6) linkage into the existing reporting system for safety events in the hospital; and (7) institutional support.
Human in the loop
The role of autonomous AI applications is intriguing because of its high efficiency; however, its use in health-care applications is too risky, and regulating human–AI interaction is necessary for success. In this context and despite advances in automation, radiation oncology remains a safety-critical domain. Human oversight is therefore essential to ensure clinical safety and maintain accountability. AI-generated outputs, such as auto-segmented structures or dose predictions, must be reviewed and validated by clinicians before use in treatment planning. Expert oversight is particularly important in situations in which errors can propagate downstream, such as incorrect organ-of-interest contours influencing inverse planning optimization and resulting in unintended dose distributions. Risks such as automation bias and silent model failure further reinforce the need for structured human-in-the-loop workflows. Clearly defined validation checkpoints, escalation pathways and audit
