Workforce training
Finally, the successful implementation of AI in radiation oncology will depend on workforce readiness across all professional roles. Clinicians, medical physicists, dosimetrists and radiotherapists must develop their foundational literacy in AI tools, understanding the capabilities, limitations and potential failure modes of these systems. Structured multidisciplinary training programmes are needed to support safe adoption, particularly as AI becomes increasingly embedded into radiotherapy workflows. Clear definition of roles and responsibilities will be essential to ensure appropriate use, oversight and interpretation of AI outputs across the radiotherapy workflow. Professional societies have begun addressing this need through dedicated educational initiatives, including AI-focused continuing education through efforts from the American Society for Radiation Oncology (ASTRO)and American Association of Physicists in Medicine (AAPM) to develop formal curricula
