Box 1 | Dos and don’ts of AI in radiotherapy
Dos
• Train artificial intelligence (AI) models on dataset clinically representative for a particular workflow (such as computed tomography simulation protocols, contouring standards or intensity-modulated radiotherapy or volumetric modulated arc therapy planning). • Use rigorously validated AI tools to support clinical decisions within defined workflow steps (for example, auto-contouring, plan optimization or image guidance). • Benchmark outputs against clinically validated reference (‘golden standard’ data) and predefined performance criteria appropriate to the intended task, such as contour accuracy, dose–volume constraints and plan for quality metrics, and integrate task-specific quality assurance checks within the clinical workflow before deployment. • Continuously monitor AI performance after deployment for model drift and changes in task-specific performance, including contouring accuracy and plan quality, with recalibration or revalidation when indicated. • Identify and evaluate failure modes using principles of failure modes using established risk-analyses methods, including failure mode and effect analysis and fault-free analysis141.
• Document AI-assisted decisions in the treatment record, including model version, inputs and any clinical edits or overrides.
Don’ts
• Do not deploy models using data exposed during training (or ‘data leakage’). • Do not deploy models trained in populations or clinical settings that are not adequately represented in the training and validation data (for example, single-institution contouring data). • Do not rely solely on an AI output without oversight from a clinical provider, and do not accept auto-contours or treatment plans without expert review. • Do not ignore model limitations, bias or variability across disease locations, and fractionation schemes, and do not overlook failure models in settings such as unusual anatomy, implants, prior irradiation or poor image quality. • Do not use AI tools outside their intended indications (or ‘off-label’ use) or validated scope (for example, applying a model developed for prostate cancer both in the postoperative and re-irradiation settings).


